  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 AM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe most active day of the period is expected today thanks to the \nfrontal boundary that brought added moisture to the region last \nnight. A subtle shortwave rotates around the monsoon high from the \nnorth during the day, sparking scattered to numerous thunderstorms \nthis afternoon over area mtns, progressing S through the evening. \n\nDew points well into the 50s and PWs in the 1.2-1.3\" range \nwill result in heavy downpours from this convection and efficient \nrain producers. The north-to-south storm motion will allow for \nstorms to redevelop over the mtns, especially the Sacs, with \nmultiple cells moving over the same location. 1-2\" of rain is likely \nover the high elevations of the Sacs and west slopes with potential \nfor up to 3\", where the flood threat is highest. The lowlands have a \nlower flood risk with a wider range of outcomes due to the more \nscattered coverage. Still, storms will be capable of producing 1-2\" \nin a short amount of time. Las Cruces and southern Dona Ana County \nhave a higher risk than surrounding areas according to the latest \nmodel data, including the REFS. The activity drifts into Mexico \nafter midnight with clearing skies overnight.\n \nStrong to severe winds are the second biggest threat after flash \nflooding due to DCAPE values approaching 2000 and a relatively dry \nsub-cloud layer. HREF data shows potential for gusts to 60 mph from \noutflows, basically over the entire CWA, although the two areas to \nwatch for are near Lordsburg and the Tularosa Basin. BLDU is \npossible along the I-10 corridor in the early evening. Small hail is \npossible as well with CAPE values of 500-1000 J/kg and about 20kts \nof bulk shear.\n\nThe rest of the forecast period is not very note-worthy as the \nmonsoon high sits over the AZ/Las Vegas region. It'll stretch into \nNM with shortwaves trying to rotate around from the north and east. \nMostly dry conditions are expected after today with storm chances \nfavoring area mtns and out west each afternoon and evening. Gusty \noutflows will be the primary threat for the lowlands. The best \nmoisture (PWs 1-1.2\") will be focused west with heavy downpours \npossible. Temps will be a few degrees above normal through next week \nwith the warmest temps expected on Tue, including possible Heat \nAdvisories for the lower RGV.",
